  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nDue to high monthly astronomical tides, water levels will \ncontinue to be high during each high tide cycle thru the \nweekend. Alongshore winds from overnight and this morning \nwill maintain a storm surge towards the coast for today's high\ntide around noon. Surge values roughly around 1 ft will bring \nus just over minor flood stage with waves around 10 ft. The Coastal\nFlood Advisory remains in effect for today's high tide. Aside \nfrom the minor flooding, beach erosion and overwash can be \nexpected.\n\nGuidance can also sometimes be too quick to lower storm surge \nbehind a departing storm, especially if winds do not quickly \nturn offshore. So will continue to monitor trends for the the \nSat afternoon high tide.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nBroad, stacked low pressure will be exiting the eastern Great \nLakes through the morning. The overnight band of precipitation \nexits northeast as drier air moves in aloft. However, low level \nwinds don't shift until later in the afternoon. This will leave \na cool, moist airmass at the surface through much of the day. \nREFS visibility probs have been showing a plume of lower \nvisibility moving into the region through much of the day, and \nthis is likely associated with fog and drizzle. \n\nBesides lowered visibility, CAD is likely to hold much of the \nday. This could create areas of freezing drizzle across the \nnorth where temperatures remain locked at or just below \nfreezing. Slick travel could result, but ice accretion without \nmore active forcing is typically at a disadvantage through \ndaytime periods. \n\nThe stagnant conditions likely don't clear until the \naforementioned low passes through the St Lawrence Valley this \nevening. A closing round of rain or showers likely accompanies \nthis, the SW winds finally scouring out the cool air locked at \nthe surface throughout the foothills and mountains.\n\nWrap around snow showers are then possible in the mountains\novernight into Saturday morning. Another weak disturbance \nbringing light rain/snow showers will be possible Saturday night\ninto Sunday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThere's good agreement in model guidance for a quick-hitting system \nto bring another round of precipitation Sunday night into Monday. \nThis will be a rain event for most of the area, except some \nlight snow accumulations are possible in the higher elevations \nof the Whites and Western Maine mountains. For rainfall amounts,\nmost ensemble guidance has less than an inch with the latest\nensemble means around half an inch.\n\nThe potentially impactful item to watch for is the low will be \ndeepening as it crosses into eastern Maine and the Canadian \nMaritimes, further tightening the pressure gradient between the  \nhigh pressure building in from the west. If we can mix well \nenough, this could bring a period of strong west to northwest \nwind gusts Monday afternoon and evening (even Mon night if we \ncan stay mixed), and there's already a decent signal in the \nensembles for gusts 40+ mph over portions of the area.\nConfidence is on the higher end for the deepening low, given\ngood jet dynamics and a sharpening trough as it begins to tilt\nnegative.\n\nBeyond Monday, Tuesday will likely remain somewhat breezy, but \nensemble guidances favors high pressure building in to keep \nconditions mostly dry in the Tuesday to next Thursday time frame. \nHigh temperatures look to cool a bit to closer to normal for Tues-\nWeds before possibly returning to above normal toward next\nThursday.",
